Date: Tue, 09 May 2017 17:09:40 +0000
Subject: Result of Fisher Exact Test and qValue
Message-ID: <8Z2RpOusfc0.Pmm3ugcoAgAJ@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/methylkit_discussion/c/8Z2RpOusfc0/m/Pmm3ugcoAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello,

In some samples calculateDiffMeth function calculates p as zero and q value could be calculated as negative. It seems a bit strange to me. It may not be a big problem since both cases we can reject null hypothesis but I'll be pleased if anyone could tell me is there something wrong  or may be I miss something linked to default multiple test correction method of methylkit. Finally do you think is it ok to write a negative q Value to a paper or saying q >0.01 makes more sense.

Thanks,

Serbulent

From uab8277113f65c05e at groups.google.com  Wed May 10 11:15:27 2017
From: uab8277113f65c05e at groups.google.com (Altuna Akalin)
Date: Wed, 10 May 2017 11:15:27 +0000
Subject: Re: [methylkit_discussion] Result of Fisher Exact Test and qValue
Message-ID: <8Z2RpOusfc0.SSn9pJLAEwAJ@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <8Z2RpOusfc0.Pmm3ugcoAgAJ@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
References: <8Z2RpOusfc0.Pmm3ugcoAgAJ@methylkit-discussion.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/methylkit_discussion/c/8Z2RpOusfc0/m/SSn9pJLAEwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

it is not expected to have negative q-values, p-values may appear as zero due to machine precision. If you are using the latest methylKit change use adjust="qvalue" method and see if it helps. If you are worried send a reproducible example. How you should present the statistics depends on journal policies, they might be OK with saying "q-value < 0.01"

Best,
Altuna

